        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Abbas the Great</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">1571 CE – 1629 CE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Born</span><span class="info-stat-val">27 January 1571 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>; third son of Mohammad Khodabanda and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khayr-al-nisa-begum">Khayr al-Nisa Begum</a>, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid dynasty</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Died</span><span class="info-stat-val">19 January 1629 at his palace in Farahabad on the Caspian coast; buried in Kashan</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Who they were</span><span class="info-stat-val">Fifth Safavid shah of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, titular governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> as a child, shah until his death in 1629</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghulam">ghulam</a> corps and the new standing army; the defeat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and the reconquest of lost provinces; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportations</a> and massacres in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>; the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hormuz">Hormuz</a> with English help; the new capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>; embassies to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>; the killing and blinding of his sons</span></div>
