    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Abbas came to the throne of a divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, its provinces held in part by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a> and its court ruled by quarrelling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qizilbash">Qizilbash</a> factions, and he went on to rule it at its military, political and economic height.</li><li>He weakened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qizilbash">Qizilbash</a> with a standing army and civil service of Georgian, Circassian and Armenian slave-soldiers, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghulam">ghulams</a>, and with it retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, while his armies killed and deported tens of thousands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>.</li><li>He moved the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> and rebuilt it, yet in his last years he had one son killed and two others blinded, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> History of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> blames the practice for the poor statesmanship of later shahs.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Abbas shaved off his beard at nineteen, keeping only his moustache, and so set a fashion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>; when an Ottoman ambassador made arrogant demands, he had the man's beard shaved and sent to the sultan.</p></aside>
    </section>

