    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1571</span><span class="rf-text">Abbas is born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>, the third son of Mohammad Khodabanda, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1589</span><span class="rf-text">He has Murshid Qoli Khan, who had put him on the throne, killed after a banquet and rules in his own right</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1590</span><span class="rf-text">The Treaty of Constantinople cedes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, Shirvan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagestan">Dagestan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>, among other lands, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1598</span><span class="rf-text">He defeats the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> and moves his capital from Qazvin to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1605</span><span class="rf-text">The Iranians win a decisive victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> at Sufiyan, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1615</span><span class="rf-text">Abbas has his eldest son, Mohammad Baqer Mirza, murdered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rasht">Rasht</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1622</span><span class="rf-text">With four English ships he retakes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hormuz">Hormuz</a> from the Portuguese and takes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1629</span><span class="rf-text">Abbas dies at Farahabad; his grandson succeeds him as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safi-of-persia">Shah Safi</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/khayr-al-nisa-begum"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khayr-al-nisa-begum.jpg" alt="Khayr al-Nisa Begum" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/khayr-al-nisa-begum">Khayr al-Nisa Begum</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Strangulation_of_Khayr_al-Nisa_Begum,_at_the_Safavid_court._Nusretname_(1584).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1584 artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Abbas was born on 27 January 1571 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>, the third son of Mohammad Khodabanda, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khayr-al-nisa-begum">Khayr al-Nisa Begum</a>, daughter of a local ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mazandaran">Mazandaran</a>. His grandfather was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahmasp-i">Tahmasp I</a>, the second Safavid shah. His father, almost blind from an eye disease, was passed over for the succession. When Abbas was barely eighteen months old his parents were sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a> and he was made nominal governor of Herat, with the Ustajlu amir Shah Qoli <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> as his guardian. He never saw his mother again and saw his father only fifteen years later. He learnt soldiering from his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qizilbash">Qizilbash</a> guardians, played polo and hunted, and was educated beside household slaves, probably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circassians">Circassians</a>.</p><p>Tahmasp I died on 14 May 1576 without naming an heir, and civil war followed. Haydar Mirza was proclaimed shah and killed at once. Ismail II, freed after twenty years in prison, murdered much of the Ustajlu tribe, including Abbas's guardian, and in November 1577 sent a man to Herat to kill the boy. The man delayed, citing the holy days, and Ismail died of poisoned opium on 24 November 1577.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Abbas the Great&#8221; cites where it covers Early life, Family tragedies and death, Consorts, Sons:</p><ul><li>David Blow, Shah Abbas: The Ruthless King Who Became an Iranian Legend (I. B. Tauris, 2009)</li><li>Roger M. Savory, Iran Under the Safavids (Cambridge University Press, 1980)</li><li>Eskandar Beg Munshi, Tārīkh-e 'Ālamārā-ye 'Abbāsī (Westview Press, 1978)</li><li>Andrew J. Newman, Safavid Iran: Rebirth of a Persian Empire (I.B. Tauris, 2006)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mohammad Khodabanda was chosen shah in 1578 but proved weak, and his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khayr-al-nisa-begum">Khayr al-Nisa Begum</a> took charge of government, even of campaigns. When she tried to dictate strategy against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qizilbash">Qizilbash</a> stormed the harem and strangled her on 26 July 1579. The murder made a deep impression on Abbas, which probably led to his belief that the Qizilbash had to be broken. In 1581 the governor Ali-Qoli Khan Shamlu proclaimed the ten-year-old Abbas shah in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> as a figurehead of a rebellion, but the quarrel was settled. Abbas's brother Hamza Mirza, the crown prince, was assassinated in December 1586.</p><p>Murshid Qoli Khan Ustajlu then took Abbas from Ali-Qoli Khan and, with an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> army besieging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>, rode with him towards Qazvin along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>, gathering Qizilbash <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">amirs</a> until the escort numbered 2,000. Mohammad Khodabanda abdicated, and Abbas was crowned in Qazvin on 1 October 1587. Murshid Qoli Khan became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vakil">vakil</a> (viceroy) and began to confine the young shah to the palace. Abbas executed three of his mother's killers and exiled four more, and on 23 July 1589 he had Murshid killed after a banquet by four Qizilbash leaders. He then made a peace with the Ottomans in 1590 that gave up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, Shirvan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagestan">Dagestan</a> and other lands.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Abbas the Great&#8221; cites where it covers Ascension:</p><ul><li>David Blow, Shah Abbas: The Ruthless King Who Became an Iranian Legend (I. B. Tauris, 2009)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/reza-abbasi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/reza-abbasi.jpg" alt="Reza Abbasi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/reza-abbasi">Reza Abbasi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_the_artist_Reza_%27Abbasi_by_Mu%27in_Musavvir,_Isfahan,_Iran,_signed_and_dated_19_April_1676.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Muin Musavvir, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Abbas used the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghulam">ghulams</a>, slave-soldiers brought mainly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, Circassia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, to offset the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qizilbash">Qizilbash</a>. He raised a force funded entirely by the crown, put ghulams in place of Qizilbash provincial governors and sent their revenues to the royal treasury. The new army had cavalry, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musketeers</a>, artillery and a bodyguard, about 40,000 men loyal to the shah, and he could field 500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> in a battle. The English brothers Anthony and Robert Shirley, who arrived in 1598, advised on its reorganisation.</p><p>With this army he attacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a> in April 1598, defeated them near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> on 9 August and held most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a> was lost in 1602. He moved the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> in 1598. Against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> he retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yerevan">Yerevan</a> in June 1604 and won at Sufiyan in 1605. In 1618 he ambushed an Ottoman army of 100,000 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardabil">Ardabil</a> with 40,000 men, and in 1623 he seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. In 1602 his forces expelled the Portuguese from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a>, and in 1622 he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hormuz">Hormuz</a> with four English ships, founding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandar-abbas">Bandar Abbas</a>. Isfahan was rebuilt with mosques, baths, colleges and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserais</a>, and painters such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reza-abbasi">Reza Abbasi</a> worked in his studios. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">Silk</a> production became a crown monopoly.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Abbas the Great&#8221; cites where it covers Abbas takes control, Reducing the Qizilbash's power and Caucasus invasions, Reforming the army, War against the Khanate of Bukhara:</p><ul><li>Roger M. Savory, Iran Under the Safavids (Cambridge University Press, 1980)</li><li>David Blow, Shah Abbas: The Ruthless King Who Became an Iranian Legend (I. B. Tauris, 2009)</li><li>Sinem Arcak Casale, Gifts in the Age of Empire. Ottoman-Safavid Cultural Exchange, 1500–1639 (The University of Chicago Press, 2023)</li><li>Andrew J. Newman, Safavid Iran: Rebirth of a Persian Empire (I.B. Tauris, 2006)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Abbas's rule in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> was marked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a> and killing. By estimate, 130,000 to 200,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a>, tens of thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circassians">Circassians</a> and around 300,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> were deported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> during his reign. In 1614 he deported 30,000 Kakhetian peasants after the Georgian kings Luarsab II and Teimuraz I defected. A punitive expedition then killed perhaps 60,000 to 70,000 or 100,000 Georgians, with twice as many deported, removing about two thirds of Kakheti's population. He executed Luarsab in 1622 and had Teimuraz's mother Ketevan tortured to death in 1624 when she refused to renounce <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. His plan to deport or exterminate eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>'s Christians has been described as genocidal. From 1604 his scorched-earth policy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> resettled up to 300,000 Armenians, and thousands died on the way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>.</p><p>Of his own family, he had his son Mohammad Baqer Mirza murdered in 1615 after rumours of a plot, then grieved. In 1621 he blinded another son, Mohammad, for celebrating his accession during Abbas's illness, and in 1627 he partially blinded the third, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imam</a> Qoli Mirza. Michael Axworthy called him a talented administrator and a ruthless autocrat with a dark twist of suspicion and brutality, and Donald Rayfield called him a murderous paranoiac when aroused. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> History of Iran blames the practice of eliminating princes for the lack of competent successors.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/safi-of-persia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/safi-of-persia.jpg" alt="Safi of Persia" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/safi-of-persia">Safi of Persia</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shah_Safi_I_of_Persia_(reigned_1629-1642)_on_horseback_carrying_a_mace(_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Abbas fell seriously ill in 1621, and his health was poor from then on. He recovered, but the illness was followed by his punishment of his son Mohammad, who was blinded and imprisoned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alamut">Alamut</a> Castle after a plot to smuggle him to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> was betrayed. In 1627 he had his last son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imam</a> Qoli Mirza, partially blinded and imprisoned in Alamut as well, for reasons that remain unclear.</p><p>Having shut out his own sons, Abbas chose as his heir Sam Mirza, the son of the murdered Mohammad Baqer Mirza, who was said to loathe his grandfather because of his father's death. He also faced continuing Georgian resistance: after Giorgi Saakadze defected in 1625 and led a new rebellion, Abbas won the subsequent war but lost half his army and had to accept Kartli and Kakheti as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> states, abandoning his plans to eliminate Christians from the area. A further rebellion in 1626 cleared Iranian forces from most of the region, and the Georgian territories kept resisting until his death.</p><p>He died at his palace in Farahabad on the Caspian coast in 1629 and was buried in Kashan. Sam Mirza succeeded him at the age of 17 as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safi-of-persia">Shah Safi</a>. In 1638 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ali-qapu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ali-qapu.jpg" alt="Ali Qapu" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ali-qapu">Ali Qapu</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C4%80l%C4%AB_Q%C4%81p%C5%AB_in_golden_time.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Amir Pashaei, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Abbas regained most of the lands lost by his predecessors and left a centralised monarchy with a regular army answerable to the shah, in place of a state held together by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qizilbash">Qizilbash</a> tribes. The Iranian–Ottoman frontier was finalised after his death at roughly the present <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and Iran–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> borders. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghulam">ghulam</a> class he enlarged went on to play a crucial role for the rest of the Safavid era and later, until the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> era. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, with the Masjed-e Shah, the Masjed-e Sheykh Lotfollah, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-qapu">Ali Qapu</a>, the Chehel Sotoun and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naqsh-e-jahan-square">Naqsh-e Jahan Square</a>, became the centre of Safavid architecture. His embassies began lasting contacts with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and Persian fashions, such as men's shoes with heels, were adopted by European aristocrats.</p><p>Historians have judged him differently. Roger Savory called him the chief architect of the modern Iranian state, a brilliant strategist whose chief characteristic was prudence, who preferred diplomacy to war. Axworthy and Rayfield stress the ruthlessness and suspicion. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> History of Iran rejects the view that his death began the Safavid decline, since Iran continued to prosper through the seventeenth century, but holds that his treatment of princes weakened the later shahs. Stories of his wisdom and fairness entered Persian folklore, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> poet Magtymguly, a century later, praised his eloquence.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Abbas the Great&#8221; cites where it covers Character and legacy:</p><ul><li>Roger M. Savory, Iran Under the Safavids (Cambridge University Press, 1980)</li><li>David Blow, Shah Abbas: The Ruthless King Who Became an Iranian Legend (I. B. Tauris, 2009)</li></ul></details>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Abbas I (1571–1629) took the Safavid throne in a divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qizilbash">Qizilbash</a> hold on the army with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghulam">ghulam</a> slave-soldiers. He retook provinces from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, rebuilt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> as his capital, and ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> by massacre and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a>, while killing and blinding his own sons.</em></p>

